# Brazen - forty-eight brass patches in seven families, each in its own range,
# each levelled, and each playing every note of that range.
#
# Families in tab order, plainest first in each and the strangest last:
#
#   high      Init, the trumpets, cornet and piccolo (8)
#   horn      horn, flugel, alto horn, euphonium, Wagner tuba (7)
#   low       trombones, tubas, cimbasso, sousaphone (8)
#   muted     straight, cup, bucket, harmon, practice and plunger mutes (7)
#   section   two to four players on one note, and the growl (8)
#   natural   valveless horns: bugle, post horn, alphorn, conch, ram horn (5)
#   odd       too small, too big, all air, a swarm, a gargle (5)
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are numbered
# as in Brazen.h's MacroDest, so #7 is pressure, #33 swell, #35 flutter and
# #37 pitch (one semitone is 0.0417). Every macro at full keeps the note
# within a few cents of where it was; the loudest drops are the Wah and Mute
# Tone macros (3-7 dB, the mute's band moving away from the notes), Loose on
# the sections (3-5 dB, the players coming apart) and Short on Brass Hits.
#
# `swell` moves the breath from note-on: positive grows into the note,
# negative is an accent that falls back into it, and a slur keeps it. Swells
# here are moderate, since a note shorter than its swell never reaches its
# own level. `flutter` chops the air at `flutterrate`; past about 0.6 it
# costs more level than it adds, so the Flutter macros go to 0.4 from
# nothing.
#
# **What makes the instruments different is the harmonic ladder, not the
# centroid.** `audition play Brazen Trumpet --ladder` prints it: a tuba is a
# fundamental with two partials over it, a trumpet an even rolloff eight or
# ten harmonics deep. Three things move it:
#
#   size      the bore, 0 a tuba's wide slow one and 1 a trumpet's narrow one
#   bell      how much of the wave comes back, and not a brightness slider.
#             The ladder peaks around 0.6 and falls away on both sides: too
#             open and nothing reflects to build a standing wave, too closed
#             and the top of it is absorbed
#   brass     the shock front, which only bites in the upper register
#
# **`tension` is not a tone control.** It is how tight the lips are, which
# decides which partial of the tube you are playing. Off 1 it detunes (0.7
# plays 97 cents sharp at F4, 1.35 plays 997 sharp) and the ladder collapses.
# Use it to pick a partial or leave it alone.
#
# **A big horn is blown hard.** `bite`, `brass` and `pressure` are the lips
# and the air, and a tuba takes more of both than a trumpet; what makes it
# dark is `size`, `bell` and `cutoff`. Blown gently the low patches read
# `hollow` -19 to -28, a tuba with its second harmonic under its third, and
# take 300 ms or more to speak. Driven, a brass patch reads -8 to -16, and
# the lips pull 10-20 cents flat, which `fine` carries.
#
# **How long a patch takes to speak** is voiced as carefully as its tone. A
# waveguide builds its standing wave over hundreds of milliseconds at low
# pitches, so a low patch with the machine's defaults (480 ms) gives a burst
# of air with no tube behind it in a phrase. Everything here speaks within
# 100 ms but the swell patches, which are named for it (Foghorn ~300 ms).
# Seating the lips already tensioned at note-on does not help: it makes the
# note take longer to build, not shorter.
#
# The natural horns let go in a tenth of a second or less. Their tunes are
# short tongued notes, and a longer release rings under the next note at
# the old pitch, which is heard as the new note out of tune.
#
# **A mute is a filter, not a volume knob.** Straight takes the body out,
# cup the edge, harmon nearly everything but the upper partials. Their
# volumes go up to 1.4 to stay level, and Straight Mute, Harmon and Muted
# Trombone vary 15-17 dB across a range: the mute's own resonance decides how
# much of each note gets out.
#
# **The players in a section have their own tubes, and they sum.** With
# `lock` high they sit within a few cents of each other, so a `scatter` of
# even 2 ms starts them at random phases and the note comes out anywhere in
# a 10-15 dB window, with the beating read as 30-60 cents of tuning. The
# locked sections start together (`scatter` 0) and come apart through
# `spread` and `drift`; Shambles and Hornet, loosely locked, scatter on
# purpose. Width 0.7 is the most a section takes before it loses level
# summed to mono.
#
# Nothing plays under note 31. Below it the tube's fundamental comes out a
# semitone flat whatever the lips and bore, so the tubas, cimbasso,
# sousaphone and Foghorn stop at G1.
#
# The demo is `role tune`, a melody that stays inside each range. On a
# range narrower than the tune its high answer is sung lower in the same
# scale, so every note is still a note of the tune's key.
#
# Levelled on `loud` to -25.6 dB, the level Init lands at. `audition sweep
# Brazen` plays every note of every range: none dead, none on the wrong
# partial. What reads oddly on purpose: Foghorn's and Hornet's `hollow` (-30,
# the swell still rising and the flutter's sidebands), Air Leak's breath.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Brazen` lists them.

machine Brazen
role    tune

patch Init family=high

patch Trumpet family=high  note=62 range=52..82
  # Narrow bore, open bell, leant on. Eight harmonics and an even rolloff -
  # the thing the tuba is not.
  set macro1 "Blow"
  set macro2 "Brassy"
  set macro3 "Breathy"
  set macro4 "Swell"
  set macro5 "Vibrato"
  set macro6 "Dark"
  set macro7 "Flutter"
  set macro8 "Growl"
  mac1_d1  #7
  mac1_a1  0.08
  mac1_d2  #10
  mac1_a2  0.15
  mac2_d1  #10
  mac2_a1  0.2
  mac2_d2  #9
  mac2_a2  0.15
  mac3_d1  #8
  mac3_a1  0.3
  mac4_d1  #33
  mac4_a1  0.2
  mac4_d2  #34
  mac4_a2  0.1
  mac5_d1  #22
  mac5_a1  0.2
  mac5_d2  #24
  mac5_a2  -0.15
  mac6_d1  #25
  mac6_a1  -0.35
  mac7_d1  #35
  mac7_a1  0.4
  mac7_d2  #36
  mac7_a2  0.1
  mac8_d1  #11
  mac8_a1  0.4
  size           0.95
  bell           0.58
  brass          0.78
  pressure       0.9
  lipdamp        0.62
  bite           0.6
  attack         0.014 s
  vibrato        8 cents
  vibratodelay   0.5 s
  breath         0.08
  volume         0.57
  fine           4 cents

